WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.080
Hollywood blockbusters have become increasingly
reliant on visual effects over the past few

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.080
decades, and recent advances in technology
have made it so feats in VFX work that were

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.320
considered impossible just a few years ago
are now well within reach.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.699
More and more studios are using CGI in films,
mostly because it's often cheaper than the

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:18.699
alternative.

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:22.890
In fact, many modern blockbusters are covered
in so many digital layers that the original

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:27.659
footage looks unrecognizable—and more often
than not, completely ridiculous.

00:00:27.659 --> 00:00:31.599
Here's what these movies really looked like
before special effects were added.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:37.449
300: Rise of an Empire
Zack Snyder's 300 made heavy use of CGI, and

00:00:37.449 --> 00:00:41.650
the technology used to create it had advanced
immensely by the time its sequel, Rise of

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.879
an Empire, came around.

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:46.890
Which, of course, meant squeezing every ounce
of that tech into the movie.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.030
Director Noam Murro told Forbes:
"It's amazing how the tools available eight

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.710
years later continue to develop.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:57.550
A major difference is CGI and the ability
to create things in post that are convincing

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.489
and complex and three-dimensional.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.040
The idea of creating a water movie without
a drop of water on the set is remarkable."

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.640
The filmmaker also revealed they relied on
some of the same techniques used in the Oscar-winning

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.670
CGI blockbuster Gravity.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:12.689
Elysium

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:17.590
Neill Blomkamp's follow-up to his acclaimed
debut District 9 didn't exactly go as planned,

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.390
despite the star power of Matt Damon.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:25.600
The blockbuster sci-fi film cost a whopping
$115 million to make, but only returned $93

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.869
million at the domestic box office.

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:32.320
But while Blomkamp took full responsibility
for the film's failings, the South African

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.900
director didn't have any complaints about
the special effects team, which had to put

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:41.090
together over three times as many VFX shots
as they had for District 9.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.880
Jurassic World

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.119
When Steven Spielberg decided to adapt Michael
Crichton's novel Jurassic Park, CGI as we

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:50.270
know it today didn't really exist.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.730
Spielberg brought on Industrial Light and
Magic and tasked them with creating living,

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:59.929
breathing dinosaurs using computer-generated
graphics, and their efforts proved revolutionary.

00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:04.259
Of course, it wasn't just CGI that brought
the inhabitants of Jurassic Park to life.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:08.770
There were a number of practical effects used
too, and ILM mixed it up in the same way for

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:10.060
Jurassic World.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:14.640
The fourth film in the franchise used detailed
white casts of dinosaur heads that would later

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.430
be layered with CGI for close-up shots, and
they used actors in motion capture suits to

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.630
make sure their movement seemed real.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.500
VFX supervisor Tim Alexander told Below the
Line:

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.690
"It gave us a new natural look for the animation.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.680
We ended up casting a person to give us a
consistency in the performance.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.650
There were individual people being that raptor.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.580
We had suits that they would put on with a
tail."

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.750
Mad Max: Fury Road

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.310
When George Miller returned to the world of
Mad Max with his critically acclaimed Fury

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:48.010
Road in 2015, audiences were blown away by
the sequel's frenetic pace and visceral action—much

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.950
of which was achieved through the hard work
of inventive mechanics who built the vehicles

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.690
used in the movie from scratch.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.950
The chase scenes were all shot for real, but
the final product wouldn't have looked anywhere

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.700
near as eye-popping if it weren't for the
VFX team.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:06.120
Led by supervisor Andrew Jackson, hundreds
of CGI artists enhanced over 2000 shots in

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.270
Fury Road, from adding characters to creating
an epic toxic storm.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:13.060
The Avengers

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.320
When Earth's Mightiest Heroes teamed up on
the big screen in 2012, the stakes were high

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.760
for Marvel Studios and their team of visual
effects artists.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.000
Jeff White, the film's VFX supervisor, told
MTV:

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.220
"With Avengers, there were so many things
to get right.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.830
We created a lot of New York City for the
film and needed to build flying shots of Iron

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.700
Man all from photography.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.970
We had to build a new Iron Man suit—the
Mark VII—and Stark Tower.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.010
We had to build the alien race.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.320
When you add all of those things up, there
are quite a few challenges there."

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.430
Green screens were used during most of the
film's action sequences, so the cast spent

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:52.900
a good chunk of time reacting to invisible
threats and taking cover from fake explosions.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.260
The biggest hurdle they faced was inserting
the Hulk into group situations.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.570
White explained:
"We wanted it to feel very natural when he's

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.410
sitting in that circle of Avengers.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.730
We spent a lot of time working on his skin
and his hair and his teeth, just to make sure

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.070
that all of that was believable."

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.400
Man of Steel

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.940
To create the illusion of flight, Richard
Donner's Superman employed wire rigs to hang

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.639
leading man Christopher Reeve in front of
different projected backgrounds.

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:22.270
The film is a classic, and the effects looked
great in the '70s, but watching it today it

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:26.599
feels as though you're flying with Superman
as opposed to him flying past you—something

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:31.849
Man of Steel director Zack Snyder wanted to
avoid when he set out to make Superman soar.

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:35.759
Snyder decided to use a handheld approach
to filming Clark's flying scenes, which meant

00:04:35.759 --> 00:04:38.419
adding in most of the effects in post.

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:42.449
Wire rigs and gimbals were used to suspend
Henry Cavill in front of green screens, and

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:44.970
the CGI team did the rest.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.830
Iron Man 3

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:52.610
When the first Iron Man movie dropped in 2008,
director Jon Favreau wasn't known for CGI-heavy

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.620
features, but advances in technology had convinced
him to change his stance.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.669
"I've always steered away from using visual
effects whenever possible…

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:03.789
That being said, in the last few years there
have been a lot of wonderful visual effects

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:07.939
movies where it's beginning to become seamless
even to me."

00:05:07.939 --> 00:05:12.689
Favreau returned for the sequel, but Shane
Black took the reins for Iron Man 3, which

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:15.560
contained some of the most challenging visual
effects yet.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:20.639
Marvel Studios head honcho Kevin Feige explained:
"We wanted the ability to be able to suit

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:23.469
up anywhere, anyhow, without a giant gantry."

00:05:23.469 --> 00:05:28.889
The answer was having Tony Stark design outlets
he injects just below the skin, not unlike

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:33.030
the comics, allowing him to call the Iron
Man suit from anywhere.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.740
To pull off the effect, each individual piece
had to be digitally added to Robert Downey

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.310
Jr.'s body after the fact.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.330
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.169
Years of gymnastics training really paid off
for Tom Holland on the set of Spider-Man:

00:05:46.169 --> 00:05:51.330
Homecoming, the character's eagerly anticipated
introduction to the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:55.810
Even with the advances in CGI in recent years,
it helps to have an actor who feels comfortable

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:00.310
jumping around in tights and doesn't mind
hanging around on wires all day long.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.330
This was the case with the film's Washington
Monument scene—but while that was indeed

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:09.669
Holland under the Spidey mask, the monument
itself was a fake, erected on a studio sound

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:10.669
stage.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:14.719
"We couldn't film at the real Washington Monument,
but we built very impressive, very large chunks

00:06:14.719 --> 00:06:16.999
of the monument for filming."

00:06:16.999 --> 00:06:21.659
Holland got up the structure with the aid
of a wire rig, though even with his background

00:06:21.659 --> 00:06:23.490
it was far from a walk in the park.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:27.720
"We did two weeks and every single shot was
upside down.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.800
And my head just took a pounding, man, from
all the blood that was rushing to it."

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.360
The Jungle Book

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:40.060
The CGI Jon Favreau used for Iron Man was
nothing compared to what he did for his 2016

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.340
live-action adaptation of The Jungle Book.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:47.270
To create an entire jungle around a human
actor, they needed to pull out all the stops.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.289
The lighting was particularly difficult.

00:06:49.289 --> 00:06:53.180
The director explained:
"It's very hard to fake light and shadow.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.550
So everything became about using panels of
LEDs to project light so if we had the kid

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:03.800
bowing before the elephants, you have panels
where we actually would pre-animate the elephants

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.660
and they would cast the shadows on the kid
in the exact right way."

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.770
This meant that 12-year-old star Neel Sethi
had to imagine the animals he was interacting

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.120
with, though Favreau was on hand to make the
experience as real as possible for the young

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:19.740
actor via puppets and actors in blue suits.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:20.909
RoboCop

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:25.830
Paul Verhoeven's RoboCop got the most out
of the special effects available in the '80s,

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:30.370
using a combination of stop-motion and prosthetic
builds to create a movie that was disturbingly

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.889
realistic at times.

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:36.840
In the 2014 reboot, Murphy gets his injuries
in an explosion that was created digitally,

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.279
but the suit he dons afterward was actually
real.

00:07:39.279 --> 00:07:43.930
"And there was a philosophy from the start
that we were going to have a head-to-toe suit."

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:48.050
The challenge was building a suit that was
mobile and easy for the digital department

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:49.650
to add onto.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:50.650
The Martian

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:55.789
Ridley Scott is no stranger to special effects,
but creating the red planet onscreen for The

00:07:55.789 --> 00:07:59.139
Martian may have been his biggest challenge
as a director.

00:07:59.139 --> 00:08:03.129
While some practical effects were used, a
huge amount of digital work was required to

00:08:03.129 --> 00:08:05.380
give The Martian Scott's desired look.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:10.699
VFX supervisor Anders Langlands told Gizmodo:
"[Ridley Scott] is famous for doing his little

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:14.309
sketches which are sort of really cool Ridley-grams.

00:08:14.309 --> 00:08:18.060
We'd ask 'What do you want the background
mountains to look like in this shot?'

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.879
And he'd sketch out a little diagram of what
they wanted.

00:08:20.879 --> 00:08:23.830
So you just literally match that and he'd
be happy."

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:28.330
A lot of time was spent finding the right
hue for the skies and arid landscapes of Mars,

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:32.770
though in the end it was a simple thing that
caused the VFX team the most problems: the

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:33.770
helmet visors.

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:38.280
"But of course glass visors would reflect
the crew, and the lights, and the sound stage,

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:43.729
so all the helmet visors you see in the film
are actually added in digitally."

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:45.320
Suicide Squad

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.930
David Ayer's anti-hero ensemble Suicide Squad
definitely had its flaws, but few of those

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:55.500
were the fault of the many visual effects
houses—18 companies in total—that worked

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:56.790
tirelessly on it.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.960
Imageworks were the ones who handled the Squad's
final battle with Enchantress, and the movie's

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.250
villain proved to be a huge challenge, according
to VFX supervisor Mark Breakspear.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:10.130
He told ARTofVFX:
"Enchantress was a unique challenge as the

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:14.750
actress had been shot without a costume as
we had to add it in later to allow it to behave

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:17.250
in a way that normal cloth or materials couldn't."

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:23.420
Breakspear later told AWN that of the 300
shots they enhanced for the film's third act,

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:26.600
dealing with the Enchantress' tattoos proved
the most difficult.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:31.530
"That was amazingly tricky to make sure it
looked like skin, but also had the translucency

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.940
that we needed to see the sub-surface tattoos."

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:39.250
The Wolf of Wall Street
Watching Martin Scorsese's ode to excess The

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:44.010
Wolf of Wall Street, the only scenes that
stick out as being possibly computer-generated

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:48.940
are the one during which Jordan Belfort's
yacht sinks and the one when a lion wanders

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:50.530
freely through his office.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:55.230
But a visual effects reel released by Brainstorm
Digital revealed that some of the most basic

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.990
shots in the movie were rendered with CGI.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.650
From sun loungers to tennis courts, Brainstorm
made plenty of subtle touches to bring the

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.560
film in line with the director's vision.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.530
VFX producer Mark Russell told Digital Arts:
"Working with Martin Scorsese, everything

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:13.130
is about propelling a story forward and contributing
to the film.

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:17.550
I feel that with his movies, there's a kind
of stylized realism to them that we have to

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.150
integrate with."

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:23.440
San Andreas
The amount of destruction on show in this

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.490
Dwayne Johnson-led blockbuster definitely
doesn't come cheap, but San Andreas actually

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:33.470
cost a lot less to make than many of the big
natural disaster flicks that came before it.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:38.740
According to Variety, the production budget
was only $114 million—roughly half the amount

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:42.010
needed to bring Roland Emmerich's 2012 to
the big screen.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:46.650
The film's visual effects supervisor, Colin
Strause, was able to keep costs down by employing

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.550
practical solutions to problems that most
visual effects companies would solve with

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.070
only computers today.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:55.500
As he explained it:
"You can make a $100 million movie look like

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.100
a $200 million movie.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.000
You can make movies way smarter.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.910
CG for the sake of CG is always a mistake."

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:04.340
They still had their work cut out for them,
though.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:09.370
Seven different companies worked together
to render 1,300 VFX shots for the movie.

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:10.580
Thanks for watching!

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:13.190
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.220
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

